{"chunk_id": "df15df034415", "text": "Vyasa explains to King Drupada the divine origins of the Pandavas and Draupadi. Long ago several Indras incurred the displeasure of a fierce form of Shiva and were destined to be born as humans, where they would perform heroic deeds before returning to heaven. These celestial beings became the sons of Pandu, while Arjuna is specifically a portion of Indra himself. Narayana also incarnates through two hairs from his body, becoming Balarama and Krishna among the Yadus. Draupadi, meanwhile, is revealed to be the goddess Sri, destined to be the shared wife of the Pandavas. Vyasa grants Drupada divine sight so he can see the Pandavas in their radiant celestial forms. The passage legitimizes the unusual polyandrous marriage and elevates the epic\u2019s heroes as incarnations acting under cosmic design.\nVyasa reveals the Pandavas are incarnations of ancient Indras and Draupadi a destined celestial wife, while Krishna and Balarama incarnate from Narayana\u2019s hairs.\nThemes: divine incarnation, destiny and prophecy, divine marriage, cosmic origins of heroes, revelation of true forms", "vector": [-0.00729568675160408, -0.02584080956876278, -0.010828403756022453, 0.001943981391377747, -0.012848040089011192, 0.012828303501009941, -0.014433486387133598, -0.005907598417252302, -0.017617536708712578, -0.0072825294919312, 0.008025913499295712, 0.013321700505912304, -0.010203435085713863, 0.006795711815357208, -0.010183699429035187, 0.025064531713724136, 0.037498120218515396, -0.013065134175121784, 0.026761814951896667, -0.010683674365282059, -0.006088510155677795, -0.018196456134319305, -0.006667428649961948, 0.029314318671822548, -0.032972030341625214, 0.03686657175421715, 0.02039371430873871, -0.016564957797527313, 0.01765700988471508, -0.016999147832393646, -0.025143476203083992, -0.0011093193897977471, 0.0022942929062992334, 0.0010024168295785785, -0.02202521078288555, -0.003772837109863758, 0.0013839766616001725, -0.02936694771051407, 